slos.description:
explain the following examples of ‘scientific paradigm’ in chemistry:
a. Phlogiston theory,
b. Plum pudding model;

describe the following terms:
a. chemical reaction,
b. chemical equation;

exemplify the formation of:
a. cations from an atom of a metallic element,
b. anions from an atom of a non-metallic element;

relate qualitatively the effect of the following factors to the rate of diffusion:
a. molecular mass,
b. temperature;

describe the macroscopic characteristics of:
a. forward and reverse reactions,
b. dynamic equilibrium;

illustrate the chemical properties of:
a. acids (reaction with bases, active metals, metal oxides metal carbonates and metal bicarbonates),
b. bases (reaction with ammonium salts);

explain the uses of hydrocarbons as:
a. fuel,
b. feedstock in industry;

illustrate the addition reactions of alkenes with:
a. hydrogen in the presence of a nickel catalyst,
b. steam in the presence of an acid catalyst;

illustrate the preparation of alkynes through the following methods:
a. dehydrohalogenations of 1,2-dihalides,
b. dehalogenations of tetrahalides;

explain the significance of naturally occurring substances in water;
a. dissolved oxygen,
b. essential minerals;

describe the composition, formation and properties of:
a. petroleum,
b. natural gas;
